#
# What the registry publishes and what the declarations add.  The registry
# publishes identity and order but never distinctness.  The declarations
# therefore state the following rule: distinct primary
# language subtag records, neither deprecated and with no Preferred-Value
# link between them, name distinct languages.
#
# The rule rests on these sentences of RFC 5646 (September 2009), quoted
# as published.
#
# Section 3, first paragraph:
#   "In addition, the meaning of the various subtags will be
#   unambiguous and stable over time."
#
# Section 3.1.5, Description Field:
#   "To help ensure that users do not become confused about which
#   subtag to use, 'Description' fields assigned to a record of any
#   specific type ('language', 'extlang', 'script', and so on) MUST be
#   unique within that given record type with the following exception:
#   if a particular 'Description' field occurs in multiple records of
#   a given type, then at most one of the records can omit the
#   'Deprecated' field.  All deprecated records that share a
#   'Description' MUST have the same 'Preferred-Value', and all
#   non-deprecated records MUST be that 'Preferred-Value'.  This means
#   that two records of the same type that share a 'Description' are
#   also semantically equivalent and no more than one record with a
#   given 'Description' is preferred for that meaning."
#
# Section 3.1.5, the same field, an example of the permitted overlap:
#   "By contrast, the subtags 'he' and 'iw' share a 'Description' value
#   of "Hebrew"; this is permitted because 'iw' is deprecated and its
#   'Preferred-Value' is 'he'."
#
# Section 3.1.10, Macrolanguage Field:
#   "This field indicates a language that encompasses this subtag's
#   language according to assignments made by ISO 639-3."
#
# The first two are what the rule is warranted by.  The third is the
# scope condition: a deprecated subtag may share a language with its
# Preferred-Value.  The fourth is the order the registry does publish; the
# generator checks that no member of the slice carries it, so that the
# resource can claim to hold no order.  None of them says that two subtags
# name two languages.  The rule is a declaration.
#
#
# Distinctness, not disjointness.  Distinctness denies that two concepts
# are interpreted as the same element; disjointness denies that anything
# lies below both.  A registry publishes no order over this slice, so only
# distinctness is available and only distinctness is needed.
#
# These declarations can be withdrawn by omitting the include.  Doing so
# reopens any definite verdict that rested on it, Compatible as well as
# Incompatible, without disputing the registry itself.
@prefix bcp47:   <https://w3id.org/odrl-kb/bcp47#> .
@prefix bind:    <https://w3id.org/odrl-kb/binding#> .
@prefix owl:     <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix xsd:     <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
<https://w3id.org/odrl-kb/bcp47/distinct> a bind:Declarations ;
    dcterms:title "Distinct primary subtags name distinct languages"@en ;
    dcterms:source <https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5646#section-3.1.5> ;
    dcterms:description "Distinct primary language subtag records, neither deprecated and with no Preferred-Value link between them, name distinct languages. Warranted by RFC 5646 sections 3 and 3.1.5; scope condition per the 'he'/'iw' example of section 3.1.5."@en ;
    bind:generatedBy bind:PairwiseDistinctness ;
    bind:origin bind:practiceRule ;
    rdfs:comment "The origin rests on the registry's own rules for its records, RFC 5646 sections 3 and 3.1.5, and not on a statement in its data."@en ;
    bind:scopeCondition "Type: language; Deprecated: absent; no Preferred-Value link within the member set"@en ;
    bind:appliesTo <https://w3id.org/odrl-kb/bcp47> ;
    bind:memberSourceFileDate "2026-08-08"^^xsd:date ;
    bind:pairCount 105 ;
    bind:states [
        a owl:AllDifferent ;
        owl:members (
            bcp47:bg bcp47:cs bcp47:da bcp47:de bcp47:el
            bcp47:en bcp47:es bcp47:fi bcp47:fr bcp47:hu
            bcp47:it bcp47:nl bcp47:pl bcp47:pt bcp47:sv
        )
    ] .